<Step id="STEP_3_MECHANICAL_CHECK">
      <Goal>Prove actual ticket structure before semantic review.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run the authoring check on every created or changed ticket. It checks required sections,
        anchors, resolvable spec references, dependencies/phases, requirement-to-scenario-to-test
        traceability, and required negative scenarios. A red result is a decomposition defect: do not
        patch the failed ticket locally. Use the retreat command `return to STEP_1_DERIVE`, rederive
        the affected node boundaries/ownership from the specs, then rematerialize and check once. If
        that remains red, stop with `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID`. Do not ask a reviewer to compensate
        for malformed artifacts.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="authoringGate">npx gennady sdd-check --task &lt;ticket-path&gt; --authoring</ToolCall>
        Consume `authoringGate`; its findings explain why decomposition must retreat.

        Then run the complete structural check over actual specs, tickets, and indexes. It checks ID
        uniqueness, dependency resolution/acyclicity, tracker sync, and declared ownership/order.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityGate">npx gennady sdd-check --all .</ToolCall>
        Consume `integrityGate`; do not proceed to semantic review while it is red.
      </Action>
    </Step>

After completing this step, and only then, READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/scaffold/steps/STEP_4_INDEPENDENT_TICKET_REVIEW.xml"). Do not preload later step packages.